Systemd Managment
===========================

1. What is systemd ?
✔ Init system (PID 1) in modern Linux
✔ Manages services, processes, targets

2. What are Units in systemd ?
Step 1: Service (.service)
Step 2: Target (.target)
Step 3: Mount (.mount)
Step 4: Socket (.socket)
Step 5: Timer (.timer)

3. Important systemctl commands
Start service
systemctl start httpd
Stop service
systemctl stop httpd
Restart
systemctl restart httpd
Status
systemctl status httpd
Enable (auto start at boot)
systemctl enable httpd
Disable
systemctl disable httpd

4. Runtime vs Permanent (VERY IMPORTANT)
Runtime (temporary)
Step 1: Changes apply immediately
Step 2: Lost after reboot
Example:
systemctl start httpd

Permanent
Step 1: Persist after reboot
Step 2: Stored on disk
Example:
systemctl enable httpd


5. What is target (replacement of runlevel) ?
✔ Group of services
✔ Similar to runlevels

6. Runlevel mapping (IMPORTANT)
Runlevel 0 → poweroff.target
Runlevel 1 → rescue.target
Runlevel 3 → multi-user.target
Runlevel 5 → graphical.target
Runlevel 6 → reboot.target


7. How to check current target ?
systemctl get-default

8. How to change default target ?
systemctl set-default multi-user.target


9. What is isolate in systemd ?
✔ Switch to another target immediately

Example
systemctl isolate multi-user.target

How isolate works
Step 1: Stops unwanted services
Step 2: Starts services of target
Step 3: System switches mode


10. Mask vs Disable (VERY IMPORTANT INTERVIEW)
Disable
systemctl disable httpd
✔ Prevent start at boot
✔ Manual start allowed

Mask
systemctl mask httpd
✔ Cannot start manually or automatically
✔ Linked to /dev/null

Unmask
systemctl unmask httpd


11. How to modify existing service ?
Step 1: Create override file
systemctl edit httpd

Example (add restart policy)
[Service]
Restart=always

Step 2: Reload daemon
systemctl daemon-reexec
systemctl daemon-reload

Step 3: Restart service
systemctl restart httpd


12. How to create a new service file ?
Step-by-step
Step 1: Create file
vi /etc/systemd/system/myapp.service

Example service file
[Unit]
Description=My Application
After=network.target
[Service]
ExecStart=/usr/bin/python3 /opt/app.py
Restart=always
User=root
[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target

Step 2: Reload
systemctl daemon-reload

Step 3: Start
systemctl start myapp

Step 4: Enable
systemctl enable myapp


13. Service file sections
[Unit] → dependency and description
[Service] → execution config
[Install] → enable/target mapping


14. Important service options
ExecStart → command to run
Restart → auto restart
User → run as user
Environment → env variable


15. How to check logs (VERY IMPORTANT)
journalctl -u httpd

Live logs
journalctl -fu httpd

Boot logs
journalctl -b


16. How to list services
systemctl list-units

Only running
systemctl list-units --type=service

All (including stopped)
systemctl list-unit-files


17. Real production scenario (service not starting)
Step 1: Check status
systemctl status myapp

Step 2: Check logs
journalctl -u myapp

Step 3: Syntax validation
systemd-analyze verify /etc/systemd/system/myapp.service

Step 4: Fix issue and restart


18. Dependency management
Example
After=network.target
Requires=network.target

Difference:
After → order only
Requires → dependency


19. What happens if service file changes not applied ?
Step 1: Forgot reload
Fix:
systemctl daemon-reload


20. Important interview traps

Trap 1: Service enabled but not started
✔ enable → only boot
✔ need start manually


Trap 2: Masked service not starting
✔ Because linked to /dev/null
✔ Must unmask first


Trap 3: isolate vs set-default
isolate → immediate change
set-default → next boot


Trap 4: restart vs reload
restart → full restart
reload → config reload only


21. Quick revision
systemctl → manage services
target → runlevel replacement
enable → boot start
mask → completely block
isolate → switch target
journalctl → logs


